    "q-334750": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334750",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4}$",
      "worked_solution": "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{2}=0.4(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{2}=5(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
